import json
from pathlib import Path
P=Path(__file__).parent/'iclr2024-sparse.en.json';d=json.loads(P.read_text());rs=d['results']
ss=rs['iclr2024-sparse-theorem2']['proof_steps'];ss[0]['body_md']=r'''Keep the source coefficient equality, $|\lambda^{(k)}|\le1$, digit bounds, witness $m_0$, and the $\delta$ bounds for positive and negative $\lambda$. The ellipsis for $0<p<1$ is recorded separately. The construction sets every low-order coefficient to zero, avoiding the author’s digit algorithm, the family $G$, and witness $k^*$.'''
ss[1]['body_md']=r'''Write $\mu_m=\bar g_0^{(m)}$. Original monotonicity gives $0\le\mu_1\le\mu_n$. Assumption 3 at $m'=1$ gives $\mu_n\le n^p\mu_1$. Split into positive-mean and zero-mean cases.'''
ss[2]['body_md']=ss[2]['body_md'].replace('positive λ',r'positive $\lambda$')
ss[3]['body_md']=r'''If $\mu_1=0$, the original assumptions give $0\le\mu_m\le m^p\mu_1=0$ for every $1\le m\le n$. Thus every layer mean is zero. Recursively at $m=k$, the counting formula has lower totals already zero and binomial coefficients zero for $k'>k$, leaving $0=A^{(k)}/\binom nk$. The denominators are positive, so $A^{(k)}=0$, starting at $k=1$. Zero means are not taken to imply zero output at every mask.'''
ss[4]['body_md']=ss[4]['body_md'].replace('The δ bound',r'The $\delta$ bound').replace('empty G','empty $G$')
ss=rs['iclr2024-sparse-theorem3']['proof_steps'];ss[0]['body_md']=r'''The source theorem occurs in Case 1, where $|\eta^{(k)}|\gg1/n$ excludes complete cancellation. Put $B_k=\sum_{|S|=k}|I(S)|$, $\eta^{(k)}=A^{(k)}/B_k$, and $\tau>0$, and let $R^{(k)}$ count the terms with $|I(S)|\ge\tau$. In this original range, $B_k>0$ and $\eta^{(k)}\ne0$, so $\tau R^{(k)}\le\sum_{|S|=k,|I(S)|\ge\tau}|I(S)|\le B_k=|A^{(k)}|/|\eta^{(k)}|$.'''
ss[1]['body_md']=r'''Apply the threshold bound to each salient term. Nonnegative absolute values give the next inequality. Finally $A^{(k)}=\eta^{(k)}B_k$ and $B_k>0$ give the absolute-value quotient. Divide by positive $\tau$ to obtain $R^{(k)}\le|A^{(k)}|/(\tau|\eta^{(k)}|)$.'''
ss[2]['body_md']=r'''Substitute the same Theorem 2 coefficient representation. Original monotonicity gives $\mu_1\ge0$, hence $R^{(k)}\le\mu_1|\lambda^{(k)}n^{p+\delta}+\sum_i a_i^{(k)}n^i|/(\tau|\eta^{(k)}|)$, which is source Eq.(6)/(54), with the same low-digit indices. At $\eta=0$, division is invalid. For an empty order, $B_k=0$ and the salient count is zero, but the ratio $0/0$ remains undefined; neither boundary is silently added to Case 1. `salient_count_bound` proves the threshold/cancellation step, `salient_bound_original_coefficients` proves substitution, `sparse_original_T2_T3` constructs witnesses from the original three conditions, and `FullSparse.theorem3` is the actual model adapter. The report distinguishes these scopes.'''
rs['iclr2024-sparse-transfer-inference']['proof_steps'][0]['body_md']=r'''Use one model $v(z)=\sum_{i=1}^n z_i$, zero input baseline, and samples $x^{(j)}=e_j$. Every sample output is $1$, so threshold classification $v(z)>1/2$ puts all samples in the same positive class and satisfies the original same-class setting. Their centered masked games are $g_0^{(j)}(S)=\mathbf1_{j\in S}$. Pure-singleton coefficient distribution gives $I^{(j)}(\{j\})=1$ with all other coefficients zero. Each sample is maximally sparse and reconstructs every mask exactly, but different samples have disjoint salient families. The model needs only $n$ singleton patterns, rather than an exponential number. This refutes the source inference without adding a pattern-budget assumption to replace it.'''
# Source issue explanations use the same unchanged formulas, classifications, and identities.
triples={
'sparse-issue-determinant-sign':('Missing cofactor sign in Lemma 3 elimination',r'For $n=3,M=2$, the Eq.(31) matrix is $\left[\begin{smallmatrix}1&2\\1&1\end{smallmatrix}\right]$ with determinant $-1$. Expanding Eq.(32) along the first column omits $(-1)^{M+1}$; iteration yields $(-1)^{M(M-1)/2}$. Only the sign changes, not nonzero determinant or full column rank.','The exact values in Eqs.(33)–(36) need repair; Lemma 3’s final kernel conclusion is retained.'),
'sparse-issue-beta-exponent':('The beta exponent disagrees with the later value',r'The formal image prints $1-q$. With $p=1,q=2$, the displayed integral diverges, while the later value is $B(1,2)=1/2$. Finite combinatorial counting repairs the proof without changing Shapley, SII, or STI statements.','This affects the beta definition and integral proof route, not the three final attribution identities.'),
'sparse-issue-beta-empty-L':('Unaddressed zero parameter for an empty L',r'For $L=\varnothing$ and $k=0$, the author calls $B(n,0)$ outside the declared positive-parameter domain. When $|L|=0$, the marked integrand is zero on $(0,1)$ and cannot uniformly integrate to $1$. The discrete weight itself is finite and needs separate boundary treatment.','The empty-family branch of the coefficient proof is repaired by the shared finite proof, retaining the final formulas.'),
'sparse-issue-taylor-validity':('The general infinite Taylor identity lacks a validity premise',r'The source says continuously differentiable functions without assuming analyticity or equality to a Taylor series. Take $n=1,r=0,x=1$ and $v(t)=e^{-1/t^2}$ for $t\ne0$, $v(0)=0$. This smooth function has all derivatives zero at the baseline, but $I(\{1\})=e^{-1}\ne0$, while Eq.(8)’s right side is zero. The counterexample concerns general Lemma 1 and does not satisfy the global derivative cutoff, so it does not refute the cutoff implication.','General Lemma 1 and the infinite expansions in Eqs.(2),(11) cannot be repaired by silently adding analyticity.'),
'sparse-issue-zero-power':('The masked-power argument omits a positive exponent condition',r'For $\kappa_i=0$, the Taylor-monomial convention gives $0^0=1$, rather than zero. The support filter only needs the implication that a masked factor vanishes when $\kappa_i>0$. Repairing this intermediate explanation does not change the support classes or final target.','This local masked-power error is separate from the general Taylor-validity gap.'),
'sparse-issue-theorem2-zero-output':('The author construction omits a zero first-order mean',r'The all-zero centered masked game satisfies the three assumptions but Eq.(41) divides by zero. This refutes only the proof step: the final identity multiplied by a zero mean can still hold. The zero-mean branch must be treated without inserting a positive-mean assumption.','The rewritten proof fully handles zero means without inferring that every individual mask output is zero.'),
'sparse-issue-theorem2-empty-G':('The constructed leading-index family may be empty',r'For $n=3,M=1,p=2$, the game $g_0(S)=|S|$ satisfies the three assumptions, with $\bar g_0^{(1)}=1$ and $A^{(1)}=3$. Its highest base-$n$ degree is $q^{(1)}=1\le\lfloor p\rfloor-1$, so $G$ is empty and the displayed maximum and $k^*$ do not exist. Lemma 3 cannot manufacture a nonzero leading block here. This is a construction gap, not a counterexample to the final existence claim.','The repaired existence construction avoids the undefined maximum rather than adding nonempty G.'),
'sparse-issue-theorem2-m0-range':('The Lemma 2 range does not cover every witness row',r'When $M<n<2M$, one has $n-M<M$, so a nonzero row obtained by Lemma 3 may lie outside Eq.(40)’s stated range. The mean-output formula extends to every retained count, with binomial coefficients zero for $k>m$. This completes the proof without adding a hypothesis.','The issue is the premise cited at Eq.(51).'),
'sparse-issue-theorem2-p-under-one':('The low-digit notation is ambiguous below exponent one',r'The allowed $p>0$ includes $p<1$, where $\lfloor p\rfloor-1=-1$ despite nonnegative digit indices. The experiments include values around $0.9$, so a new $p\ge1$ restriction would be unfaithful. The finite-family reading $J=\{i\in\mathbb N:i<\lfloor p\rfloor\}$ is empty there. If the printed constant digit remains separate, assigning it zero also works. The project witness handles any finite low-digit family without claiming the original ellipsis literally defined negative indices.','The index-family scope is disclosed while preserving the original displayed expression.'),
'sparse-issue-eta-zero':('Division by the cancellation ratio at zero or an empty order',r'An empty order gives $0/0$. Nonzero effects with exact sign cancellation give $\eta=0$, making Eq.(57)’s division undefined. Theorem 3 is situated in Case 1 with $|\eta|\gg1/n$, which already provides a nonzero context. The proof is faithful in that range and is not enlarged to complete cancellation.','Complete cancellation is outside the usable quotient domain; the Case 1 derivation adds no new assumption.'),
'sparse-issue-noise-variance':('Randomness alone does not imply the variance multiplier',r'The source does not state independence and equal variance of the noise terms. If every $\epsilon_T$ is the same nondegenerate random variable $Z$, centering gives $\epsilon_T-\epsilon_\varnothing=0$, so every noise dividend vanishes. The claimed multiplier fails. Reading fully random as IID would require explicit source justification, and the empty centered dividend is zero rather than one copy of the noise variance.','The variance conclusion requires a separately recorded interpretation of independence; it is not silently inserted.'),
'sparse-issue-parity-empty':('The parity example conflicts with the centered empty value',r'The empty set has even size, so the displayed parity definition gives $u(\varnothing)=-1$, whereas the paper’s centered convention requires $u(\varnothing)=0$. The source example does not exist under these simultaneous definitions. Renaming u as a raw game or changing its empty value would change the example.','The source-symbol scope conflict is retained separately.'),
'sparse-issue-transfer':('Sparse reconstruction of each sample does not imply transfer',r'For $n\ge2$, use one model $v(z)=\sum_i z_i$, zero input baseline, and samples $x^{(j)}=e_j$. Each induced centered game is $g_0^{(j)}(S)=\mathbf1_{j\in S}$, with one nonzero singleton dividend and exact reconstruction on every mask. Their salient supports are disjoint. All outputs are $1$, so threshold $v(z)>1/2$ classifies them in the same positive category. Only $n$ patterns are needed. The original contradiction has no global pattern-budget premise.','The original same-category inference is refuted without adding a new pattern-budget assumption.'),
'sparse-issue-decomposition-notation':('Component names and denoising split signs disagree',r'The source gives both components the AND name. In the denoising equations, both include $+\gamma$, making their sum $u-\epsilon+2\gamma$ rather than the denoised output. These intermediate notation/sign errors can be repaired while preserving the final decomposition $u=u_{and}+u_{or}$.','The issue concerns the intermediate algorithmic formulas, not a changed final split.'),
'sparse-issue-asymptotic-sparsity':('Polynomial cancellation does not imply same-order sparsity',r'For $n\ge3$ with $n\equiv2,3\pmod4$, let $q=\binom n2$ be odd. Assign second-order coefficients $\pm1$ with counts $(q-1)/2$ and $(q+1)/2$, giving total $-1$. The model $v(z)=\sum_i z_i+\sum_{|A|=2}c_A\prod_{i\in A}z_i$ at the all-one input and zero baseline has $A^{(1)}=n,A^{(2)}=-1$ and zero higher interactions and mixed derivatives. Its order mean is $\mu_m=m-\binom m2/q$; the increments are $1-m/q>0$, and $\mu_m/m=1-(m-1)/(2q)$ is nonincreasing. The original three assumptions hold with $M=2,p=1$. With fixed $\tau=1/100$, $R^{(2)}=q$ and $\eta^{(2)}=-1/q$, polynomially small despite every second-order term being salient. Theorem 3’s bound $100q$ still holds.','This refutes the extra Case 2 same-order inference, not the neighboring empirical most-cases description, Theorem 3, or total polynomial sparsity relative to $2^n$.'),
'sparse-issue-example-H-arithmetic':('Appendix H omits the singleton x3 contribution',r'The formal table prints zero for mask $\{3,4,5\}$, but $v=x_1x_2x_3+x_1x_2+x_2x_3+x_2+x_3$ at the all-one input and zero baseline gives output $1$ there. The sum over ten third-order masks is $19$ and the mean is $1.9$. The original comparison with second-order mean $1$ remains true.','Only the table entry and derived mean are repaired.'),
'sparse-issue-reconstruction-index':('A switched sum uses S containing L instead of T',r'The preceding line requires $T\supseteq L$, but the next line prints $S\supseteq L$, which cannot filter the varying T terms and does not produce the stated binomial count. Restoring the correct bound or using the full subset bijection proves the same reconstruction claim.','This is a local proof-index error, not a false reconstruction theorem.'),
'sparse-issue-marginal-free-L':('A grouped cardinality sum leaves L unbound',r'After grouping by size $l$, L is no longer a summation variable; the sign exponent must depend on $l$ to use binomial cancellation. The original free-L expression is retained. The shared finite-difference proof avoids it while keeping the original lemma and all boundary cases.','This repairs the local grouping step without changing the marginal theorem.'),
'sparse-issue-STI-free-S':('The critical-order STI proof leaves an environment S free',r'The original environment S has been grouped by $q=|S|$, but the denominator still contains a free S. The next integral step needs denominator $\binom{n-1}{q}$. The shared finite factorial-convolution proof directly yields the original critical-order weight while retaining all three branches.','This is a local index error in the proof, not a changed STI proposition.'),
'sparse-issue-T2-parameter-domain':('Theorem 2 leaves some parameter domains unstated',r'The source does not repeat Lemma 3’s $M<n$ in Theorem 2, so that restriction cannot be inserted. The new construction covers $M=n$. Real base-$n$ logarithms need $n>1$, and the displayed binomial denominators are nonzero for $M\le n$. No explicit positive-M convention was found: if $M=0$ is allowed, the all-zero centered game at $n=3$ satisfies the three assumptions, but the empty sum $\lambda=0$ contradicts $\lambda\ne0$. That reading has a separate counterexample, without inserting $M>0$ into the unconditional source proposition.','The full existence construction is verified on the displayed positive-order expression domain; the zero-order reading remains separately refuted.'),
'sparse-issue-lemma3-copied-row':('Eq.(33) copies the n−2 matrix row',r'The formal second row outside its first column repeats $n-2$ in Eq.(33), whereas adjacent-row differences in Eq.(32) require $n-3$. The original array remains in the full transcription. Correct elimination or the completed finite-difference induction proves the original kernel conclusion without using that erroneous matrix.','The copied row and the missing cofactor sign are separate proof errors.'),
'cvpr-issue-bow-table7-probability-scale':('Table 7 is incompatible if the adjacent probability scale continues',r'The page-17 footnote beside Table 6 says in this example and uses a probability output. Table 7 on page 18 gives singleton value $6.568$ and pair value $-13.481$. If the same probability scale applies, Möbius differences lie respectively in $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$, so the values are incompatible. The footnote does not unambiguously extend to Table 7, and the section does not state whether its scale changes. The values are therefore not unconditionally refuted. The formal images were checked, ruling out an extraction error.','Only conditional compatibility of the adjacent output convention and Table 7 is at issue, not real-valued reconstruction or unconditional table data.')}
d.setdefault('issues',{})
for name,(title,analysis,impact) in triples.items():d['issues'][name]={'title':title,'analysis_md':analysis,'text_md':analysis,'impact_md':impact}
P.write_text(json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
